---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Sessions">
  <h1>Sessions</h1>
  <p>
    A session is a stored refresh token. Identity treats every refresh
    token as a session row in the datastore, with metadata (device name, IP, user
    agent, timestamps) attached for the user-facing session list.
  </p>

  <h2>Lifecycle</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Issue.</strong> On successful login (password, OAuth, passkey,
      QR-approved, TOTP-completed), Identity creates a
      <code>RefreshToken</code> row. The raw token is returned
      to the caller; only a hash is stored.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Use.</strong> Calling <code>RefreshToken(refreshToken)</code>
      verifies the hash, deletes the old node, and issues a new
      access/refresh pair. This is <strong>refresh-token rotation</strong>:
      a stolen refresh token is invalidated the moment the legitimate
      client uses theirs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Revoke.</strong>
      <code>Logout</code> deletes one session.
      <code>RevokeSession</code> deletes a specific session by ID
      (admin/self).
      <code>RevokeAllSessions</code> and <code>SignOutEverywhere</code>
      require password confirmation and delete all sessions for the user.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Expire.</strong> Sessions older than
      <code>GATEWAY_REFRESH_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code> are rejected at refresh
      time. There is no background expiry sweeper — expired rows linger
      until the user touches them.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What's in a session row</h2>
  <Code code={`Session {
  string session_id     // refresh-token node id (NOT the raw token)
  string device_name
  string ip_address
  string user_agent
  Timestamp created_at
  Timestamp last_used_at
  Timestamp expires_at
  bool current          // true on the session whose token made the request
}`} lang="proto" />

  <h2>Listing sessions</h2>
  <p>Authenticated user lists their own sessions:</p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/ListMySessions \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{}'`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Revoking sessions</h2>
  <Code code={`# Revoke one session (the user can do this without re-auth)
curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/RevokeSession \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"sessionId":"sess_01HZ..."}'

# Sign out everywhere — requires password confirmation
curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/SignOutEverywhere \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"password":"correct horse battery"}'`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Access-token revocation</h2>
  <p>
    Revoking a session invalidates its <em>refresh</em> token. The associated
    <em>access</em> token continues to verify until <code>exp</code> — up to
    15 minutes by default. If you need instant kill, lower
    <code>GATEWAY_JWT_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code>; there is intentionally no
    distributed access-token blocklist (downstream services should not have
    to talk to Identity on every request).
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
